WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.280
Film Courage: What would you tell a 20-year-old
director who is just getting started?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.720
Or they don’t have to be 20, they can be
younger.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:14.389
Julian Shaw, Writer/Director/Actor: I got
it.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.309
Start making stuff.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.960
You have an iPhone in your pocket, it’s
perfectly good.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.820
Start practicing your craft.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:27.660
But figure out what really turns you on and
just be yourself.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.759
Maybe you do things in an unconventional way
and some people might think that’s a weakness

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:34.870
but actually that can become a strength.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:41.340
I realized at film school certain kinds of
movies that I love to watch I wasn’t very

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.530
good at making.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.870
Just because I love Paul Thomas Anderson doesn’t
mean I could make a film like him.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:54.230
You can get really enamored with these giants
of Hollywood and think well I’ve got to

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.390
make something like my hero.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:02.109
And I think actually the weirder things about
my sensibility, that I used to think were

00:01:02.109 --> 00:01:04.000
a weakness, are my strengths.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.080
That’s what makes me original.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.520
That’s my voice.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.550
The stronger and more unique your voice is
actually the more commercial your work will

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:14.280
be right?

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.860
Because it’s pure and it touches people.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.329
So just try and find your voice.

00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:20.610
And you can do that for no money.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.780
You can do that with your iPhone.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.740
Just keep making things, keep pushing and
you will get there, you will get there.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.220
Film Courage: I find that interesting…so
yes…just because you love a certain style,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.790
don’t try to emulate it because it may not
be your skill?

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.680
Julian: Kind of, we all go through a thing
where we fall in love with Hitchcock or whoever

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.810
Scorsese…these incredible people and then
you look at what you’re making and you can

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:49.040
be so dispirited because of course it’s
not as good as Hitchcock or Scorsese and realistically

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.170
it probably never will be.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:51.170
But that’s okay.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.689
Don’t try to be them, try to be you.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:02:00.790
And I probably intellectually knew that but
as time has gone on I really feel that.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.159
I feel that in my stomach.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:09.810
I’ve just got to be me and my unique, weird
(whatever) take on things is good enough and

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:16.110
actually it’s a lot better than if I’m
trying to copy filmmakers that I admire but

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.700
that just comes with experience.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:25.570
Film Courage: Looking back on everything now,
why are you still working in the film industry?

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.500
Julian: I just…I mean…I just love it.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.500
I don’t know what else to tell you.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.990
It’s a cliche but it just lights me up.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.480
There is nothing that satisfies me more.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.590
I just showed the film in Brooklyn (USE ME
movie) like a couple of days ago and hearing

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:49.940
the audience gasp in shock or laugh or have
whatever emotional reaction they had it really

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.110
does all make it worthwhile.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.950
You do this because you’re trying to connect
with others, I mean that’s the word that

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.010
underscores the soul.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.530
Weirdly you go into a very isolated space
and you’re working on it for a few years

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:07.370
and you’re in an edit room or you’re writing
and a lot of moments of being alone and isolated.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:14.541
But it’s that communal atmosphere that you
are always thinking about and how is this

00:03:14.541 --> 00:03:16.490
going to play for an audience.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:22.260
When you get the joy of that happening it
really makes all of the ups and downs worthwhile

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.260
and that’s why I do it because that’s
what inspired me when filmmakers gave me that

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.190
good feeling of joy in my heart and I hope
to keep doing that for the rest of my life.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:32.880
It’s the only thing that I want to do.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.670
Film Courage: Whenever I go into a movie theater
and there is no one there I think “I hope

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.060
at least one person arrives.”

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.480
I don’t want to be the only one watching
this movie alone.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.930
Julian: Even if it’s just one person and
it doesn’t even have to be a movie theater.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:47.680
It could be someone watching it on a streaming
platform but if you can get across that thing

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.100
that was in your head to that person, that’s
cool.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:55.280
It’s super hard but the challenge is what
makes it worthwhile.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.310
So I don’t know what to tell you I just
irrationally, totally love it with all my

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.340
heart despite how hard it is.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:07.340
Maybe because of how hard it is and I think
I’ll keep doing it until the end.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.240
Film Courage: What are the stories you want
to tell and be involved in?

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.370
Julian: Honestly I just want to entertain
people.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:19.040
I just want people to walk out of the films
that I am acting in or directing feeling like

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:20.930
they are walking on air.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:27.660
Maybe I’ve taken them to a really dark place
but they feel more alive by the end of it.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.350
So actually I just want to give people joy.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.840
I think that’s really what it’s all about
for me.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:42.260
And if you can inspire discussion about tough
or controversial issues or maybe issues that

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:46.080
are quite unresolved in society, that’s
great.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.510
But number one, entertain people and even
if that is all you do, that’s enough.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:53.840
That’s really enough.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.850
Giving people joy is a pretty amazing thing.

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.420
So I want to be part of productions that do
that.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.500
Film Courage: Did you see THE ROOM?

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.560
Julian: I did see THE ROOM.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.340
I saw THE ROOM in Portland [Oregon] with Tommy
Wiseau…and you know what?

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.370
It’s kind of a masterpiece.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.870
I mean…okay is it a zero star movie or a
five-star movie.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:17.220
You could argue that it’s a five-star movie
because of the audience engagement and the

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.220
joy.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.590
If that’s your pure metric it’s one of
the best movies I’ve ever seen and people

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.740
throwing [stuff] at the screen.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:25.310
Film Courage: Imitating lines.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.920
Julian: Right and again I said professional
wrestling.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:34.130
People think that is very uncool but it brings
tears to my eyes, it gives me joy.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:40.580
That’s awesome and you feel alive when you’ve
got that smile on your face.

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:43.780
Film Courage: That’s really cool.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.780
He [Tommy Wiseau] was in the audience?

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.150
Julian: He came and did a Q&amp;A.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:48.400
It was so cool.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:49.400
It was really great.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.110
Why do you ask about that?

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:56.720
Film Courage: I was just thinking of the one
scene in the version with James Franco [THE

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:04.550
DISASTER ARTIST] where you see Tommy pull
up in the limo and just the night of the first

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:11.210
screening for filmmakers and there are so
many emotions but it’s this stamp of approval

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.560
of “Okay, I did it.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.730
And now people are coming to see it.”

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.020
And sometimes it’s a bigger deal than it
really is and sometimes it’s the opposite

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.590
but you don’t know that going into it.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:23.452
And so that scene [in the limo the night of
the screening] and he drives around [the theater]

00:06:23.452 --> 00:06:24.600
again it’s funny.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:31.060
And especially if you’ve ever had a first
movie screening you kind of know it’s such

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.060
a huge deal.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:33.670
Julian: I was anxious as heck.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:34.670
Film Courage: Right.

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.860
I don’t know why I brought this up, it just
popped into my head as we were speaking.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:45.090
And I pictured Tommy Wiseau (the real Tommy
Wiseau) in a limo…or the James Franco [THE

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:51.272
DISASTER ARTIST] version going around and
around the theater the night of the screening.

00:06:51.272 --> 00:06:54.550
Julian: The other thing I could add is you
never really quite know what is going to connect

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:55.550
with people.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.639
I’m not sure if I even have an answer to
say about this but you just kind of keep making

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.150
stuff and it surprises you what does hit and
it might not be the thing that you expect

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.480
but if you just keep making stuff.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.639
I didn’t really have a formal answer, it’s
just what I’ve noticed.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:14.120
I don’t know what to say about that.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.740
You never know how an audience is going to
respond but you have to be connect to keeping

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:21.590
on telling stories and then eventually there
will be something that seems to land.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:22.700
Film Courage: Right.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:26.980
Maybe there is no answer because you never
know…I mean to have this viral video [Julian

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:27.980
was in a viral video in 2011 with “It’s
Time” which is currently at 16 million views].

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.889
Julian: Oh that totally fell into my lap and
then it was like “Okay?

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:31.889
Wow!”

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:33.080
And it opened all of these doors.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.480
Film Courage: Sure, you just don’t know.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.110
Julian: But if you keep making the effort
an putting yourself out there it’s more

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:41.419
likely you are going to find something that
will connect.

00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:43.450
That’s really all you can control.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.700
Film Courage: Did Tommy [Wiseau] do a Q&amp;A?

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:46.770
Julian: He did.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.220
Film Courage: Anything interesting?

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.400
Julian: Oh my gosh.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.260
Well he was loving the whole thing and I just
realized it’s a pretty good business model.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.770
I mean he’s selling t-shirts and selling
posters and I was like “This guy is really…this

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.300
is a profitable movie now.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.960
This is a super-profitable movie.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.520
So he kind of owns it and he was playing a
character, right?

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.980
I mean he is playing “Tommy.”

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.160
But he’s very smart for doing that.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.040
He does it well.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.450
It was cool.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:14.650
I really enjoyed it.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.500
And THE DISASTER ARTIST was very funny too.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:20.650
Film Courage: It’s very funny but then there
are parts that are very true for anybody who

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:22.440
is trying to make a film.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.410
Julian: Frighteningly true.

